    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d82">

                <pb facs="0062" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1939v05/pg54"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.852/11</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1756</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-16">September 16,
                        1939</date>.<lb/>[Received September 25.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my telegram No. 327 of
                    September 14, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d82-f1"/> regarding the offer of the
                    German Government to sell German boats now in Brazilian ports, I have the honor
                    to report that the Minister for Foreign Affairs remarked to me yesterday that if
                    Brazil were not successful in obtaining the boats promised them by the
                    Moore-McCormack Steamship Line, the Brazilian authorities would buy the German
                    boats. I said, “what will the English and French say?” He replied, “Oh, we can
                    arrange that: the British are being excessively polite to us these days.”</p>
                <p>In that connection, the Director of Exchange of the Bank of Brazil observed this
                    morning that conversations in that regard are going on with the German
                    authorities.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jefferson Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
